Commit 1e91845d6f37d8bce671d62c14f4390324da3659 (release 0.9.5), MIT licence (LICENSE, Copyright (c) 2026 mickys).
Imported: src/ and vt/ and LICENSE. Left out: the upstream tools, trace interposers, test streams, benchmark data and
documentation (about 100 MB), none of which the library needs.

Changes made here, in this order of importance:
  vt/nvencvt.c   NVEncVT_RegisterDecoders(): registers only the decoders, for the decode service (appended before
                 load_conf).
                 EnableLowLatencyRateControl: declared as a selection property of the H.264 and HEVC encoders, accepted by
                 SetProperty / CopyProperty and listed among the supported properties. A session that sets it is real-time
                 with no B pictures. Without the declaration VideoToolbox drops the encoder from the candidates of any
                 client that asks for low-latency rate control (FFmpeg's low_delay, Sunshine), and answers -12902.

  src/hevcparse.c, src/h264parse.c, src/av1parse.c  Fixes found by running the parsers under AddressSanitizer with a coverage-guided
                 fuzzer (tools/test_nvenc_parsers.py holds one case for each; to be sent upstream):
                 - ue() clamps to 2^28: a code up to 2^32 - 2 became a negative int, passed "id > max" checks and indexed the SPS / PPS
                   arrays (an out-of-bounds write when the parameter set was stored);
                 - scaling_list_data(): pred_matrix_id_delta checked against 0..matrixId before it indexes the list array;
                 - st_ref_pic_set(): the inter-predicted lists were written past 32 entries before the limit was checked;
                 - hrd_parameters(): cpb_cnt_minus1 limited to 31 (a stream that had ended still ran 2^28 loop rounds);
                 - hevc_parse_sps() / h264_parse_sps(): the sizes, bit depths and block sizes of the specification's ranges are
                   checked before the SPS is stored (16 * width overflowed int); av1 decode_unsigned_subexp_with_ref() shifts unsigned.

  Blackwell and the other Turing chips (tools/test_nvencvt_blackwell.py), written from public sources only:
                 - src/rm.c: Blackwell's classes 0xcfb7, 0xcfb0, 0xc96f (open-gpu-kernel-modules clcfb7.h, clcfb0.h, clc96f.h);
                 - src/blocklinear.c: gob_offset / gob_untile_plane / gob_layout_for, the Blackwell8Bit and Blackwell16Bit plane
                   layouts and the rule that picks one by element size (Mesa NIL, src/nouveau/nil/tiling.rs and copy.rs, MIT);
                 - src/dec.c: the H.264 decoder untiles in the card's layout;
                 - src/channel.c: methods that do not fit the push buffer are dropped and the submission fails (it wrote past
                   the mapping when a channel stopped fetching); the buffer is reused after a completion the decoder saw,
                   because clc96f.h documents no GPGet word;
                 - vt/: card kinds for Blackwell (H.264 decoder only) and TU10x / TU116 (decoders only), by PCI device ID.
                 Not run on a card yet. Nothing registers on these cards unless NVMTL_NVENCVT names the process or is 1.

Where upstream's own facts come from (read before calling this library clean of NVIDIA's closed driver): the decoders'
picture setups are open-gpu-doc's nvdec_drv.h. The ENCODERS' per-picture control blocks (src/h264enctmpl.h,
hevcenctmpl.h, av1enctmpl.h) are byte tables that upstream generated from traces of NVIDIA's Linux driver encoding on
the same cards, and src/enc.c, la.h and two decoder comments cite such traces for buffer sizes and method use; the
input layout in blocklinear.h was measured from a working client's surface. That is observation of a driver's output,
not disassembly, but it is the same kind of source as NullMoth's own encoder libraries.

